  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a nanosensor’s optical emission can be tuned by its local environment. The central idea is that an isolated nanoparticle has a particular electronic structure, but applying either a non-zero crystal field from surrounding ligands or a non-zero electromagnetic field lifts degeneracy in its energy levels and changes the light it emits.\n\nThe left side shows a ligand-coated nanosensor being excited and then emitting. Beneath this, simplified energy-level diagrams indicate that crystal fields split the metal d orbitals into lower and higher sets, separated by an energy gap Δ, enabling characteristic radiative transitions after excitation. In the middle, the isolated nanosensor is shown with more degenerate levels. On the right, an external electric or electromagnetic field similarly perturbs the electronic states, producing shifted energy levels and altered emission.\n\nThe top-right schematic emphasizes that as field strength increases, the energy levels move apart or cross, changing the effective bandgap-like separation. The bottom-right plot translates this into spectra: emission peaks shift across wavelength with changing field strength, while maintaining similar peak shapes. Overall, the figure conveys a sensing mechanism in which local chemical coordination or fields modulate nanosensor energy levels and therefore its fluorescence color.",
